registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design’s search-quality
checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate
for the prescribed search procedure; the rule does not say whether a prediction computed from a
flagged search is itself incomplete. Table 21 therefore adds two stricter readings, computed from a
map of the searches each prediction cell requires, directly or through the recorded dependency chain
of its evaluation record (references, attack banks, and warm starts); the map is released with the
scorer, and no reading was chosen as official after the outcomes. If a flagged required search makes
a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, Q3-d, and Q3-e are not assessed: 6 predictions are
fulfilled, 8 fail, and 4 are not assessed (counting direct inputs only, Q3-e stays fulfilled: 7, 8, and
3). If, as a convention of this sensitivity, it makes every prediction ineligible except those that score
a confirmatory decision (Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b; the design keeps these
decisions but does not say how predictions about them are scored), Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-d are
also not assessed: 5, 4, and 9. No reading turns a failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a
registered decision; the marked rows of Tables 19 and 20 are those a stricter reading changes, and
their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The predictions differ in stakes and
are not independent, so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are
informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five
markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3’s and
C∗ ’s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the
descriptive reading).



