10     What the results show and what they do not
What the two tests support together. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3
searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3’s regis-
tered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule’s frozen truthful reference;
it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities, which would need a
matched comparison with replacement disabled. The registered comparison does not establish that
margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with
C∗ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the
same ordering for every variant on every market. At ρ = 0.2, pacing slightly raises the measured
gain while changing efficiency little; at ρ = 1, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the
allowance and causes R3’s collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.

What they do not support. The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross ef-
ficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference
against R3 and C∗ ; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory
mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empir-
ical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished,
and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C∗ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to
the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot
bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The
fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the
frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision
unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.

Exact but costly checks. The model’s audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost
charged to the operator’s budget. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object,
that a committed computation ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model’s audit
cost only if the operator funds both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need
separate incentive accounting. Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the
questions; if the cost of a proof fell close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled
audits would weaken. This discussion is outside the registered tests and changes none of their
claims.

Limits. The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither
equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition 1 establishes a
profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every
studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets
come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development.


11     Conclusion
For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement
removes the ban’s continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches
yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-
and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search
flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within


                                                   23
the registered margin under both Q1 families. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained
welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about 0.840 against all three core rules and earn less than the
truthful reference against R3 and C∗ . The registered W1 search yields gross efficiency near 0.997
for both rules. Exploratory episode-level mixtures selected on the evaluation records yield, with H4,
estimated efficiencies of 0.932 for R3 and 0.921 for C∗ ; public D2+H3 mixtures meeting the empirical
training constraint yield 0.945 and 0.922, respectively; all at non-negative estimated evaluation
gain. The registered W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies; the
population profitability of the selected mixtures remains unestablished. These conclusions concern
frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The
record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed
predictions, will be released with the paper.

Availability. The paper artifact will have its own release commit and archive hash. It identifies
the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag protocol-v2 and preserves the original tagged-code
report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed,
and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the devia-
tion evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc
analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-
scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, and the paper sources. One com-
mand (docs/revision/deviation/reproduce_deviation.py) checks the artifact against the tag
and reproduces the second test’s report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper’s
second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability out-
put, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in docs/revision/paper-v2,
docs/revision/posthoc, and docs/revision/audit, and the first test’s records and verdict are in
the release as recorded in October 2026.


A     Parameters and definitions
First test and headline market: providers i = 1, . . . , 10 have qimax = (0.60, 0.65, . . . , 1.00, 1.00), bi =
(0.40, 0.38, . . . , 0.22), ai = 0.02, ki = 6, [vmin , vmax ] = [0.6, 1.4], σ = 0.05, β = 0.05, ρ = 0.2. Fresh
markets of the second test: N , J, T , σ, β, ρ, and vmin as at the headline; q max ten sorted draws from
U [0.55, 1.0]; bi falling linearly in the quality rank between bhi ∼ U [0.34, 0.46] and blo ∼ U [0.18, 0.26]
with noise U [−0.02, 0.02]; ai ∼ U [0, 0.05]; ki uniform in {4, . . . , 8}; vmax ∼ U [1.2, 1.6]. The focal
provider is at the median position. The first-best surplus S ∗ of a seed is the sum over rounds of the
maximum-weight assignment of jobs to capacity slots at full effort.


B     Proofs
Lemma 1. Shading saves sg. Each audited escrow is refunded with probability d instead of d0 .
Given K, independence of delivery noise gives the probabilities 1 − (1 − d)K and 1 − (1 − d0 )K of
at least one failure; the continuation loss is incurred once. Taking expectations over U gives the
identity.

Corollary 1. A fresh identity faces the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states, up to
relabelling, as the surviving identity without a failure; immediate free replacement attains it, so
CU = 0.



                                                      24
Lemma 2. R3’s priority list is ordered by age group, newest first. Within a group, independent
continuous audit keys make the selected subset uniform among the group’s jobs; delivery noise is
independent of selection.

Theorem 1. The spell length is geometric with mean 1/qE ; since survival to round t is determined
by earlier rounds, the expected spell profit is rE /qE . Adding the recovery’s rewards and entry charges
and dividing by the expected cycle length gives Π̄ by the renewal-reward theorem [29]. A cycle’s
surplus is at most (rE − h)/qE + ϵ ED; dividing by 1/qE + ED and using ED ≥ L gives the bound.

Proposition 1. With CU = 0, A′ (0) = sb − ϕ(τ /σ) σ −1 E
                                                              P
                                                                j∈U Mj ≥ s (b − vmax ϕ(τ /σ)/σ), since
Mj ≤ vmax and K ≤ s.


